Then there's the question mark block, which you can hit for rewards.

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:31.120
And then there's just kind of like the after the question mark block's been hit, those solid brown blocks that you know won't do anything.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:32.880
In this game, they all look the same.

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:36.000
And I understand.

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:40.740
I was just trying to describe like the actual act that you're doing in the game.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:47.800
I totally understand that Mario is different because it is clearly communicated to you what block does what.

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:55.440
There is a weird scenario though, and I know we keep harping on like this first thing, but I think this is as far as most people are going to make it.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:18:01.120
So for the ability to go to any room of your choice is possible.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:05.860
Like you don't have to start in the very first room that we keep calling room one.

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:13.140
You can go to the third room and get to that part where you need the spring of the shoes and not have the shoes yet.

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:16.580
And you can still get through that stage without having the shoes.

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:24.560
But you would have to know that a very particular block on the right side, only one of them, you could shoot all the other ones, nothing will happen.

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:30.560
But only one of them will actually burst open and make a hole so that you can skip the part that would require the shoes.

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:32.060
And you can go to the next segment.

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:38.460
Now, how you would ever know that is also, again, just limited down to guesswork.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:47.740
But also, if this whole game has got like this Metroid-style collectibles thing where, you know, like, oh, you don't need all the upgrades in the game, but they'll certainly help.

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:54.480
In this one, it seems like, yeah, you don't need the shoes, but then actually it turns out later you still need the shoes.

00:18:55.060 --> 00:19:00.620
Like, it will – it's only there to tell you, like, because you couldn't get past the second part of the stage.

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:02.520
It would just let you pass the first part of the stage.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:06.820
So this way you can get to the exit, but it wouldn't actually let you, like, beat room three.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:10.120
You would have to then be like, huh, wonder what went wrong in that room.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:11.820
Oh, maybe I need a pair of shoes.

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:41.120
Yeah. Joe, it sounds like you were able to make it to some of the boss fights, though, did you?

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:46.800
I made it to the first boss fight, which was, I mean –

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:47.980
What a disappointment.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:50.300
So, yeah, you just shoot at it.

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:51.840
I mean, you know, it moves back and forth.

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:59.080
You shoot at it. It is still, I think, a little challenging just because it's so – like, at that point, your health's probably already pretty low.

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:01.300
And, like, you got to hit it right in the head, I think.

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:03.020
You have to hit it directly in the head.

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.960
And it's a dog with, like, bat wings.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:11.720
Yeah. I just assumed it was just, like, a pretty generic dragon thing.

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:18.620
But then after that, once I beat it, I – honestly, that's when I'm like, okay, I'm not – I'm not going any further than this.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:24.840
But, yeah, it was – honestly, I don't really have too much more to say.

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:28.240
It hops around. It drops a little crystal ball afterwards.

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:29.700
And then you move on to the next area.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:35.000
And then you put down the game and you look up a video so you can talk about it on the podcast.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:42.480
Well, you know what's funny is I'm glad you brought up the boss thing because I think the most important thing is finding the boss.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:48.340
Because I don't know about you, but it's not communicated that, like, he would be in a window.

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:49.540
It's just a window. Right.

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:52.360
Yeah. I went into the window.

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:56.320
Like, it was actually the first place I went into.

00:20:56.540 --> 00:21:01.840
Like, I just – I didn't even realize I pressed the up button and I was transported into this place.

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:03.960
And it's just, like, empty.

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:09.180
Like, you show up and it looks like there's, like, a trigger fault in the game.

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:10.280
Like, a glitch happened.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:15.240
Like, because you're in a place that you're obviously supposed to use as an arena and nothing's happening.

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:20.940
So, I mean, yeah, it's interesting that they left it there for you to just find.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:25.180
But it doesn't seem to – I don't know why it's there.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:26.140
I don't know why. Yeah.

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:33.260
I – that was the other thing is because I also went in once before the dragon was there or whatever he is was there.

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:39.320
And then when I went in later after I had done the stuff that we talked about earlier, bought those different things,

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:43.100
I'm not sure what exactly I did that triggered the boss to be there.

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:47.540
Like, thinking back on it, something that I did made it so the boss was there.

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:50.280
But, like, even that was unclear even after I did it.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:59.020
Yeah. Yeah, I think now that you mentioned that, I don't actually – because I'm surprised to hear from Sean that you were able to get in that room but nothing happened.

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:00.960
Because I only went in there and he just happened to be there.

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:03.120
But I must have done something but it's not communicated.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:11.180
I think the trigger is you have the shoes and you have the –

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:13.140
The mini? Yeah, the little potion.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:15.400
Right. I think that's what it is, that you have those things.

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:20.620
But there's no, like, in-story mechanic that makes it make sense that those would make the –

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:21.900
You know, that's what bothers me.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:25.720
It's like, oh, you just collect those things and you can move forward because that's how video games work

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:31.920
and not because you use the shoes to, I don't know, get to the spot where he is or something like that.

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:36.620
Just to go back to the castle overworld thing that I was talking about, and I don't know if that's –

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:37.740
It's like a hub, right?

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:39.460
A hub would be a better word. Yeah, that's a better descriptor.

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:48.780
Yeah. So this castle hub where all the rooms live and stuff, you know, at first it is pretty boring and everything –

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:52.300
Even the rooms are colored the same as the bricks, which I don't think is helpful.

00:22:52.560 --> 00:23:01.820
But there are a couple of, like, neat things like that well that you have to try to, like, get to from the second floor to go down and access the well room,

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:07.800
which is like a cool little dungeon castle underground area.

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:10.360
Does it look different than the regular levels?

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:11.760
It does, yes.

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:16.240
Oh, okay. It's like a dark blue, and I think it even has different music.

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:25.540
But there's – you know, like, you go down the well and then it takes you to, like, a fire room, which is just, like, a room that's on fire.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:29.720
And you would think I'm just making all this up because, like, why would a bottom of a well have a room on fire?

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:32.480
It's an oil well.

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:42.820
And then at the third floor, it's a little bit different because there's these shrines that you can get access to.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:48.840
But otherwise, it's mostly just, like, poking in and out of doors again and stuff like that.

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:54.980
And then the fourth floor is just the final challenge boss room style thing.

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:59.700
And I think that that's very weak in terms of, like, explorable secrets in a hub.

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:03.300
Yeah, I mean, have – what else have we seen this?

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:10.060
I mean, I keep saying that, like, oh, they did something new, so it should be applauded.

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:16.600
But, I mean, I guess because I had no opportunity to see these things, I can't really give them too much credit.

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:21.000
But, I mean, we haven't seen hub secrets before, so at least they did it.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:32.260
I don't know. Yeah, but that's, like, kind of saying rewarding Metroid for, like, you know, the first area is just the hub and then you take the elevators to get to the real levels.

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:40.800
Like, that – I mean, like, that's a more interesting use of a space, you know, than just having it be a castle that you just kind of go in and out of doors.

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.900
And then, oh, wait, there's this well and some shrines.

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:45.900
But other than that, you don't get anything.

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:57.260
Like, I think it would be cool to find other secret rooms that have just power-ups that aren't necessary for progress, but that you could get these power-ups to be stronger than usual.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:59.160
Some kind of, like, RPG elements like that.

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:00.760
Sure. Yeah.

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:03.620
And you mentioned earlier you were talking about Super Mario Bros.

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:12.080
3. And, I mean, obviously this is me taking, like, what's widely considered the best game on the NES and comparing it to this.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:23.680
But, like, that would be a good, like, example of – you know, there's plenty of stuff to do just on that little, like, board game overworld map that, like, at first seems like it's just to get you from level to level.

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:27.680
But there's all kinds of little secrets and hidden things there.

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:35.960
Yeah. And there's things, like, that you buy in the shop, like Excalibur, the sword, but then it just – it's just your bubbles are stronger.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:37.760
Like, you don't actually wield a sword.

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:49.180
Yeah. Um, and there's, uh, there's a feather that, uh, you would think would help you, like, fly, but instead it just lets you use an elevator in one specific room.

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:53.040
You've never used, like, a feather key card?

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:55.940
No. Is that a thing?

00:25:57.260 --> 00:25:59.760
I love that you at least sort of believe me.

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:04.060
I like the idea that there is a feather key card and that's, like, a cute reference.

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:05.620
Instead it's just garbage.

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:07.560
Uh, all right.

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:09.100
So, yeah, Joe, you have something?

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:10.580
Well, one more thing on the overworld.

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:14.720
Did you guys get the, like, lightning storm thing that was just going on?

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:16.720
Yeah. I thought, like, I triggered something.

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:18.380
Um. So did I.

00:26:18.580 --> 00:26:21.620
Does that just happen randomly? It happens if you linger too long out there.

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:25.440
I don't know why it's punishing you for just being on the overworld map.

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:26.280
Um.

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:32.400
But I guess if you hang out there too long, a thunderstorm happens and lightning will chase

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:33.560
you forever now.

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:38.860
Because I remember going into a level, finishing it, and then thinking, surely that must be

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:41.180
over. And then it's still happening.

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:45.640
It's just, I don't know what they're trying to do with anything that they're doing in this

00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:47.060
game. Yeah.

00:26:47.300 --> 00:26:49.260
There were probably, it seems like they were like, you know what's cool?

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:51.740
Lightning. And then they just put it in the game.

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:57.520
But it bothered me a little bit because also when you would go through a door, you know,

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:00.000
there's like a good second of animation there.

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:01.980
It can hit you. Even once you're gone.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:05.860
Once you're gone through the door and there's nobody on screen, if the lightning goes through

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:08.200
the space that you were, you'll lose health.

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:10.100
Same thing with when you're coming out of the doors.

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:13.720
So I kept getting hit by when I had no control over my character.

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:15.300
That's a great topic.

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:16.940
You know, taking damage.

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:19.060
Because there's a lot of problems with that.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:24.780
First and foremost being that you don't have any invincibility frames.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:31.580
If you're touching anything, you just take damage until you move out of the way.

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:38.380
And whether it's lightning or other opponents in the rooms.

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:45.320
There was another game that we played like this that I don't know if you guys remember

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:47.140
that it had a similar problem.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:52.900
I don't know if this is the one you're thinking of, but was it Karate Champ?

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:54.160
I think so.

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:56.700
Maybe? That's the one where the people would come up behind.

00:27:56.920 --> 00:27:58.360
Was that Karate Champ? No, Karate Kid.

00:27:58.760 --> 00:27:59.860
And you're right, Joe.

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:02.900
Oh, I was thinking of the one where they come up and they hug you.

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:04.540
No, that's Kung Fu. That's really, really one.

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:05.500
That's Kung Fu. Kung Fu.

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:08.840
That's right. There's so many Karate and Kung Fu games.

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:10.840
But I don't know if that's the one you're thinking of.

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:11.840
That's the one that came to my mind.

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:16.600
I think that that's just something that has been established already.

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:23.040
It's a good design choice to allow the player to at least get out of harm's way once they

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:25.860
take damage. And they just ignored that.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:28.380
Yeah, that can be a little frustrating too.

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:34.780
Yeah. And in the opposite realm of like your lifespan and stuff like that, there's those honeycombs

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:40.000
that will actually give you an extra life bar so that you can continue to extend your

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:41.100
life. Yeah.

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:42.420
But they're just hidden randomly.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:44.300
There's no... They're in every single stage.

00:28:44.300 --> 00:28:47.720
You just got to try and shoot every block to find it if you really want to have more health.

00:28:48.460 --> 00:28:54.500
Exactly. And you need to do that because once you die once, that's the game.

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:03.460
And there's music boxes hidden in each level too, which bring you to a bonus stage that

00:29:03.460 --> 00:29:05.220
made no sense to me.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:11.380
It was just like this band was playing and you have to collect these music notes that

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:12.780
just pop up from the ground.

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:17.220
And it doesn't really care whether you collect a lot or a little or in between.

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:20.240
You're not rewarded or harmed in any particular way.

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:24.740
So what I read, and only because I read the manual because I never got to play any of these

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:33.840
levels, was that if you collect the music notes and you want to keep getting sharps, like

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:37.920
sharp notes, but if you get flat notes, then you're penalized.

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:41.720
So maybe you just collected everything you could and didn't notice.

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:42.600
Which is true.

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:47.720
Yeah. And maybe what I think you're supposed to get some reward.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:49.580
At least that's what I saw in the manual.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:52.300
So maybe they weren't clear with that either.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:57.660
Is there like, even if, like, even outside of the game, is that like, even if it's not

00:29:57.660 --> 00:30:02.360
explained, is there some like reason that sharp notes would be good and flat notes would

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:06.040
be bad? Well, sharp notes are historically proven to be the better sounding note.

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:08.420
Is this true?

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:09.600
No. I don't think so.

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:13.960
Historically? Everybody believes everything Sean says and he's just lying.

00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:16.480
Well, we all know about feather keys now, thankfully.

00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:26.380
Totally. The other hidden item in every single stage is that honey bee that just like flies away after

00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:27.680
you discover it.

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:30.020
And that's actually the Hudson Soft logo.

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:31.700
Hudson Soft being the makers of this game.

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:33.000
And that's the bee, Sean.

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:35.300
Did you see it? That's the bee I keep talking about.

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:37.980
I saw the bee, Mike, and I recognized the bee.

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:42.020
Okay, good. I'm glad that you'll think of me every time you see the bee.

00:30:42.100 --> 00:30:42.980
Yeah, I actually will.

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:46.020
Yeah. Whenever you're playing this game again in the future.

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:52.560
I guess this counts as a hidden thing in each stage, but there's also the keys to get out

00:30:52.560 --> 00:31:01.480
or hidden in each stage, but they also only appear when you collect either $4 or reveal 15 blocks

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:02.980
or kill five enemies.

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:04.940
Yeah, that I didn't understand.

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:10.840
I knew that had to be something, like there had to be some prerequisite to finding the key

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:15.340
because the first time I played the second level, I was just stuck.

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:20.380
I didn't know why I couldn't find a key, even though I felt like I broke everything.

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:22.800
Right, right. It makes sense.

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:28.240
And I just, I have to shout out to a website because we're all talking about like looking up guides

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:34.740
and stuff like that, but I always look for the pure dedication of people who love old NES games

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:38.500
to try to get like the best way to inform myself about these games.

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:42.360
And I found this website called Mylon's Secret Website,

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:46.360
which is clearly a fan-made website by this one guy.

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:54.360
Oh, I love it. But it's a work of art because he has like every single level graphed out on graph paper,

00:31:54.700 --> 00:31:58.380
you know, like mapping out like this door and this is where the things are hidden.

00:31:58.540 --> 00:32:03.460
And it's just, it truly is beautiful that someone cared this much about the game

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:05.520
that we are now taking a huge dump on.

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:06.540
It's crazy.

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:12.900
It's crazy that whatever game it is, you'll find somebody that has that kind of dedication to it.

00:32:13.100 --> 00:32:18.940
And I'm not going to pretend to understand it, but I sure as hell can appreciate it.

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:23.000
Let's all say something nice about Mylon's Secret Castle.

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:24.720
You first.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:26.260
Yeah, go for it, Joe.

00:32:26.260 --> 00:32:30.440
I actually do have a good, a real compliment for it.

00:32:30.520 --> 00:32:35.040
I think that aesthetically, it's designed nicely.

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.480
And some of the enemies have good designs too.

00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:40.960
Okay. That actually is something genuinely wholesome.

00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:48.180
Yeah. I mean, I think it's questionable whether or not it's truly good or just passable,

00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:49.720
but I'll allow that.

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:51.160
Sean, something nice. Something nice.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:55.020
No, no, no. I'm just saying that if you're going to say something nice, at least make it true.

00:32:56.520 --> 00:32:57.640
We're not putting it out.

00:32:57.640 --> 00:32:59.520
You don't think it's a nice looking game?

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:01.300
That's a participation award, dude.

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:03.260
No, no, no.

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:05.420
I'm giving it a genuine compliment.

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:08.740
Just because I don't like the game doesn't mean I can't find good things about it.

00:33:09.780 --> 00:33:11.040
My nice thing.

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:12.300
Yeah, go for it.

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:19.940
My nice thing is that hidden inside this game, like all those items and all that cash and stuff,

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:23.200
hidden inside of this game is a great game.

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:30.300
You just have to actually, you know, design the game in a way that all of this stuff actually makes sense.

00:33:30.480 --> 00:33:32.200
And is it just random guesswork?

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:49.680
Yeah. I think if you used a slightly different sprite for breakable blocks, maybe had a little bit more explanation as to what was needed.

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:52.440
Maybe some cool cut scenes.

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:54.240
Maybe making it open world.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:56.460
I knew that was coming.

00:33:56.980 --> 00:33:59.460
I don't even think it needed like a tutorial or anything.

00:33:59.700 --> 00:34:04.700
I think it just needed to guide the player a little more like on the nose.

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:06.620
And some people would have hated that.

00:34:06.760 --> 00:34:11.900
But I think just the idea of how complicated it is to even understand the shoes thing, right?

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:13.500
The very first collectible in the game.

00:34:13.640 --> 00:34:16.240
One of many. There's going to be many things you have to get in this game.

00:34:16.240 --> 00:34:23.420
And just I can't overstate like it's so weird that they don't care if you find out about it or not.

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:29.060
Like the fact that you have to push that weird block that you would never think to do unless you read the manual.

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:31.500
And it's like, yeah, the manual is kind of guiding you.

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:37.200
But I care about games that actually guide you through like the cleverness of the game design itself.

00:34:37.200 --> 00:34:43.240
If there were even like an arrow there telling you like, hey, did you know you can move these blocks if you want to?

00:34:43.400 --> 00:34:44.320
Like there's a shoe in here.

00:34:44.420 --> 00:34:45.240
Like things like that.

00:34:45.500 --> 00:34:49.580
Some sort of context that would make you try these things.

00:34:50.060 --> 00:34:55.740
Exactly. The game is like a collection of these answers that you'll never find with no questions.

00:34:55.960 --> 00:35:02.360
There's no actual like, yeah, there's no actual reason to think like, oh, how would I get this?

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:04.060
Maybe it's by getting shoes.

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:05.540
Like there's no context to that.

00:35:05.540 --> 00:35:31.180
Because I've been I've been trying to think of like, you know, how we got from here with just unexplainable reasons for for the character to do things in a game to like 2013 or something with Final Fantasy 13, giving you a recap of everything that happened, like every 10 minutes and making every every battle a tutorial battle.

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:35.420
Somewhere along the line, we went from one extreme to another.

00:35:35.540 --> 00:35:39.760
And just like anybody that plays games doesn't want either of these extremes.

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:49.760
We just if you're going to have like mysterious things that you have to figure out on your own, this have to make sense within the game logic or in real time logic.

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:50.980
You know what I mean?

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:54.540
Like, I don't know where we went wrong.

00:35:55.220 --> 00:36:13.280
I absolutely agree with you, because I think it's why games like Mega Man X and Super Mario Brothers one, like it's a reason why they're so talked about all the time is because they set up exactly what you need to do without ever telling you like push this button to do this or hey, just so you know, this is a thing you can do.

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:15.380
Like it just explores it within the level.

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:42.260
Hey, listen. So you couldn't have thought that Mylon's secret castle was the only secret castle that Mylon would be exploring, right?

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:49.700
I mean, obviously, this is a love, a loved franchise that that has had many castles, right?

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:52.020
It's a it's an iffy answer there.

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:56.320
Mylon is not a mascot by any sense of the imagination.

00:36:56.600 --> 00:37:01.200
Hudson Soft will go on to do much better things like make the Mario Party games.

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:11.880
So they didn't they didn't do too much with the character, but they did port Mylon's secret castle to Game Boy, which makes sense considering how bare bones it is.

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:13.380
I'm sure it could fit on a Game Boy.

00:37:14.300 --> 00:37:22.860
And they later in 1996 on the Super Famicom, which is pretty late in the Famicom, Super Famicom journey.

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:29.680
They released a game called Do-Re-Mi Fantasy Mylon's Doki Doki Adventure.

00:37:31.200 --> 00:37:33.200
So that still has the musical thing.

00:37:33.320 --> 00:37:34.740
What does Doki Doki mean?

00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:36.700
I would love to know.

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:37.760
I have no idea.

00:37:38.020 --> 00:37:40.580
I don't want to have it spoiled, actually, so I don't love to know.

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:44.740
Don't tell me. But yeah, Do-Re-Mi, that's still musical.

00:37:45.200 --> 00:37:51.380
Hopefully the characters actually talk in music in this one because, you know, it's got that 16-bit architecture.

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:57.340
I'm pretty sure there's no talking whatsoever because it kind of just became a generic platformer.

00:37:57.400 --> 00:37:58.880
There's no castle anymore.

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:02.240
It's just him through like a like a rainforest.

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:04.800
Okay. Yeah, that doesn't make sense at all.

00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:06.940
Yeah, but it's pretty colorful.

00:38:07.120 --> 00:38:08.640
There's no castle at all? There's no castle.

00:38:09.860 --> 00:38:10.700
That's a ripoff.

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:17.440
Yeah, you would think he would like be leaving a castle in the beginning of this game and then being like, well, I'm off to the jungle.

00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:20.180
But he's not.

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:22.460
All right, what else do I have here?

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:28.340
I have some Mylon no Hoji Zora Jaban puzzle Kimokoyu.

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.580
Okay. Nailed that title.

00:38:32.020 --> 00:38:36.940
But that's basically what Mylon's been reduced to at this stage of his life.

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:43.420
He's like a bubble bubble type game where he shoots these bubbles because that's what he was doing.

00:38:44.200 --> 00:38:47.460
Three colors in a row and they break off just like in bubble bubble.

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:51.200
I mean, is that even really being reduced at this point?

00:38:51.380 --> 00:38:57.460
Sounds like an upgrade. You think that that would be the equivalent of like Mario just being Dr.

00:38:57.620 --> 00:38:59.700
Mario. Except Mario's good.

00:39:00.740 --> 00:39:02.160
No, I know. I'm saying like...

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:08.140
Like maybe if you just completely change the format, you have a chance of like just starting from scratch and making something that's fun.

00:39:08.440 --> 00:39:11.220
Yeah. Well, it didn't take him off the...

00:39:11.220 --> 00:39:13.280
It didn't like bring him back to life.

00:39:13.340 --> 00:39:14.860
So I assume he died there.

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:26.220
Yeah. He is a playable character in Saturn Bomberman, which is that one weird Bomberman game on the Sega Saturn where 10 players can play at the same time.

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:28.540
Wow. I didn't even know I had the capability to do that.

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:31.600
Oh, that's like the best thing about that whole system.

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:34.740
People always talk about Saturn Bomberman.

00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:38.680
If you can find 10 people in a room and put them all at the same TV.

00:39:39.900 --> 00:39:45.800
That's cute. What I would like to see Mylon in is like, you know, Marvel versus Capcom or something like that.

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:49.400
Absolutely. Or, yeah, Marvel versus Hudson Soft.

00:39:49.420 --> 00:39:54.840
Yeah. Or, you know, maybe I'll just make him in WWE 2020, you know?

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:56.320
There you go.

00:39:56.540 --> 00:39:57.600
I'm sure he'd look great.

00:39:58.060 --> 00:40:03.120
And the last thing I found out about was this manga called Famicom Rocky.

00:40:04.220 --> 00:40:06.840
And it was this like...

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:12.520
It's described on the Giant Bomb website because they did a deep dive on it.

00:40:12.680 --> 00:40:19.020
It's described on Giant Bomb as basically Rocky the Movie, which is why it's called Famicom Rocky.

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:27.820
But instead of him like training for a boxing fight, he's just training to play like old Famicom era video games.

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:32.460
Okay. And so Mylon's Secret Castle is one of those games that he plays.

00:40:32.840 --> 00:40:34.540
So that's great.

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:38.120
Yeah. I mean, you know, it's just a really weird thing.

00:40:38.260 --> 00:40:39.380
I'll link it in the show notes.

00:40:39.500 --> 00:40:42.120
I think it's always worth finding out about these weird mangas.

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:42.960
Because this is like...

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:46.280
Famicom Rocky is like their Howard and Nestor, I guess.

00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:47.760
What is Howard and Nestor?

00:40:48.280 --> 00:40:54.200
Oh, Howard and Nestor was the Nintendo Power comic strip for like the first hundred issues.

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:55.620
They had a...

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:58.480
Howard was Howard Phillips, who was the...

00:40:58.480 --> 00:41:02.500
Like the guy who played every Nintendo game ever.

00:41:02.780 --> 00:41:03.620
That was his job.

00:41:03.740 --> 00:41:06.280
And Nintendo really marketed him as like this cool thing.

00:41:06.420 --> 00:41:09.180
Like, look, this guy gets paid to play video games.

00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:16.440
Wow. So Famicom Rocky would be like the, you know, the shonen alternative.

00:41:17.360 --> 00:41:19.800
Okay. I don't have...

00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:22.280
I'm stretching right now, so I have nothing else to say.

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:25.020
Let's get into the essential games list.

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:33.360
Joe, you said some nice things about Mylon's Secret Castle, so I'd like you to go first.

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:35.740
Please tell us your essential games list.

00:41:35.740 --> 00:41:41.300
Well, I am currently watching a video of a lot of it right now, and I do stand by.

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:43.420
I think there are some cool designed enemies here.

00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:45.940
And also, I'm noticing there...

00:41:45.940 --> 00:41:49.920
Like, you mentioned that this game, there are like things that had potential to be good.

00:41:51.260 --> 00:41:57.120
And that is a really nice way of putting it, because really, I don't think any of the gameplay in this game is good.

00:41:57.120 --> 00:42:10.120
But I'm watching a level here where there's like ice blocks, and if you stand on top of them, they melt, and you have to kind of make it right through, which is a cool idea for like a mechanic of like getting through a sort of maze.

00:42:10.640 --> 00:42:13.960
But it doesn't look like it actually functions in any cool way.

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:21.020
So what I've concluded is that this game was made by a bunch of people who are all good at what they do.

00:42:21.240 --> 00:42:29.000
One person who like made the puzzles, but a different person who made the answers to the puzzles, and a different person who made the assets as the person who designed where they go.

00:42:29.180 --> 00:42:32.080
And these people were not allowed to speak to each other as they made the game.

00:42:32.360 --> 00:42:33.760
Like, that's what it feels like.

00:42:33.820 --> 00:42:39.620
There are a bunch of cool little elements that just make no sense together, that don't work together.

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:44.120
So yeah, I mean, I don't think I'm going to surprise anyone.

00:42:44.280 --> 00:42:46.400
But yeah, this is nowhere near an essential game.

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:47.600
I wouldn't give it a play either.

00:42:48.360 --> 00:42:54.200
Got it. And so Sean, on the opposite end of the spectrum, you refuse to say anything nice about this game.

00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:55.860
No, you know what, Mike? So please tell us your vote.

00:42:55.980 --> 00:42:59.880
Like, now that I think about it, like this game performs really well.

00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:04.460
It always runs at like a silky smooth 60 frames per second.

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:06.880
I didn't count any frame drops when I was playing.

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:09.400
Very few glitches.

00:43:10.640 --> 00:43:15.280
You know, when I ran, when I pressed right, I ran to the right.

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:16.680
When I pressed jump, I jumped.

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:21.860
Like, they definitely got all the basic things down to make a video game.

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:26.940
But other than that, like, I can't really find anything really good to say about this game.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:28.600
So it's not essential.

00:43:28.880 --> 00:43:32.620
Yeah, I mean, really, at the end of the day, it's not Grand Theft Auto 6.

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:36.680
It's not. So it's not going to, that's the next game that's going on our essential games list.

00:43:37.220 --> 00:43:43.600
I could go on for a while about, like, why this game isn't Super Mario Brothers.

00:43:43.600 --> 00:43:49.340
And I think that that's important because Super Mario Brothers is like the platformer that we hold up to the skies right now.

00:43:49.800 --> 00:43:54.040
And so that's why you need to understand why these kinds of games don't get on the essential games list.

00:43:54.040 --> 00:44:02.980
But I feel like I'd just be wasting time because I'm pretty positive almost all of our audience would agree with us that this is not an essential game.

00:44:03.160 --> 00:44:06.600
There's just no way that this belongs on the same list as the other greats.

00:44:07.400 --> 00:44:14.800
Whether there's actually a good game hidden in there, like I was alluding to earlier, I think it would require a massive overhaul.

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:16.640
I think there's, like, a few nice ideas.

00:44:16.780 --> 00:44:19.140
But you could say that about a lot of video games.

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:23.460
So I was, like, I was half serious, half joking on that.

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:32.160
But I think even a ROM hack version that, like, spells things out a little more wouldn't necessarily find a more enjoyable experience.

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:36.960
You'd have to really look into this game again and think about what you were trying to do with it.

00:44:37.080 --> 00:44:38.860
And for that reason, it's not essential.

00:44:39.100 --> 00:44:41.520
All right, guys. That's the episode.

00:44:42.060 --> 00:44:43.680
We can leave the castle.

